and waiting around for build,

00:02:15.470 --> 00:02:18.985
test, and deploy are
clearly not the fun parts.

00:02:18.985 --> 00:02:20.860
Didn't Google once face
these sort of issues?

00:02:20.860 --> 00:02:23.530
And how were we able to
remedy that at such scale?

00:02:23.530 --> 00:02:24.940
ADAM SELIGMAN: Google has
built a lot of great software

00:02:24.940 --> 00:02:27.010
to run these services
that billions of people

00:02:27.010 --> 00:02:29.650
use every day, so a lot of--

00:02:29.650 --> 00:02:31.030
a couple of decades
of experience

00:02:31.030 --> 00:02:33.730
went into how do we help Google
developers become incredibly

00:02:33.730 --> 00:02:36.700
productive so they
can spend an hour--

00:02:36.700 --> 00:02:39.970
six hours on average of
writing software every day

00:02:39.970 --> 00:02:41.550
and absolutely
minimize everything

00:02:41.550 --> 00:02:43.300
else in the chain of
getting to deployment

00:02:43.300 --> 00:02:45.460
and getting that service
out to production where

00:02:45.460 --> 00:02:49.090
a small number of users can
test it as a canary deployment

00:02:49.090 --> 00:02:50.480
and start giving you feedback.

00:02:50.480 --> 00:02:52.930
So we're trying to build
that into our cloud.

00:02:52.930 --> 00:02:56.982
We've seen the rise of DevOps
practices out in the industry,

00:02:56.982 --> 00:02:58.690
but now we're building
into our products,

00:02:58.690 --> 00:03:01.660
with products like Cloud
Code, like Managed Kubernetes,

00:03:01.660 --> 00:03:06.820
and CSP, Cloud Run, our
new serverless offering.

00:03:06.820 --> 00:03:09.670
Just really reducing
the barriers anyone,

00:03:09.670 --> 00:03:12.010
like a Google engineer,
can build code

00:03:12.010 --> 00:03:14.213
and get it into production
as easily as possible.

00:03:14.213 --> 00:03:15.130
RETO MEIER: Fantastic.

