00:05:13.196 --> 00:05:15.540
RETO MEIER: And let's check
in with Joanna for a closer

00:05:15.540 --> 00:05:18.210
look at the network
postcard experiment,

00:05:18.210 --> 00:05:22.228
and to understand
why speed matters.

00:05:22.228 --> 00:05:24.270
JOANNA SMITH: Hi, I'm in
the Dev Zone once again,

00:05:24.270 --> 00:05:26.110
but now I'm with
Terrence Ryan, who

00:05:26.110 --> 00:05:27.990
one of my favorite co-workers.

00:05:27.990 --> 00:05:30.060
I'm not going to lie about that.

00:05:30.060 --> 00:05:32.810
So this demo in particular--
it's a digital postcard.

00:05:32.810 --> 00:05:33.830
Is that right?

00:05:33.830 --> 00:05:35.580
TERRY RYAN: Yeah,
that's kind of the idea.

00:05:35.580 --> 00:05:38.940
The idea is you take
a selfie or picture,

00:05:38.940 --> 00:05:42.540
and we will bounce it around
our network, our private network

00:05:42.540 --> 00:05:44.500
all around the world and
print it out for you.

00:05:44.500 --> 00:05:46.452
So the pictures that
you get over here

00:05:46.452 --> 00:05:48.910
are pictures that have traveled
around the world digitally.

00:05:48.910 --> 00:05:49.830
JOANNA SMITH: Oh,
my gosh, and so

00:05:49.830 --> 00:05:51.038
that's what these stamps are.

00:05:51.038 --> 00:05:51.580
Each data--

00:05:51.580 --> 00:05:52.920
TERRY RYAN: Each data center--

00:05:52.920 --> 00:05:54.940
each data center, we
have a stamp for it,

00:05:54.940 --> 00:05:56.760
and it doesn't stamp it until
it's in that data center.

00:05:56.760 --> 00:05:58.350
JOANNA SMITH: Now, when you
talk about a private network,

00:05:58.350 --> 00:06:00.180
you mean the actual
physical cables

00:06:00.180 --> 00:06:02.593
and data centers that Google
built around the world.

00:06:02.593 --> 00:06:04.260
TERRY RYAN: Yeah,
exactly, the same data

00:06:04.260 --> 00:06:06.780
centers and same infrastructure
that powers Search

00:06:06.780 --> 00:06:10.110
and Mail also, under
the covers, it's

00:06:10.110 --> 00:06:12.240
available to Google
Cloud customers

00:06:12.240 --> 00:06:13.810
as their private network.

00:06:13.810 --> 00:06:16.480
So whenever you talk between
one machine and another machine

00:06:16.480 --> 00:06:18.780
on Google Cloud, it's always
traveling on our network.

00:06:18.780 --> 00:06:20.640
It never leaves the
network, and so you

00:06:20.640 --> 00:06:22.140
get much better
performance in terms

00:06:22.140 --> 00:06:24.778
of being able to
communicate and security,

00:06:24.778 --> 00:06:25.820
because it's not leaving.

00:06:25.820 --> 00:06:27.180
It's not on the public internet.

00:06:27.180 --> 00:06:28.350
JOANNA SMITH: Oh, my
gosh, this is actually

00:06:28.350 --> 00:06:30.183
one of my favorite facts
about Google Cloud.

00:06:30.183 --> 00:06:32.460
It's a fun fact, which is
we built an infrastructure

00:06:32.460 --> 00:06:34.865
to power our Search, and then
we started opening that up

00:06:34.865 --> 00:06:36.240
to other people
so that you don't

00:06:36.240 --> 00:06:37.500
have to reinvent the wheel.

00:06:37.500 --> 00:06:39.750
But it's really important
that we make it easy to use.

00:06:39.750 --> 00:06:41.482
Was this pretty easy to set up?

00:06:41.482 --> 00:06:42.440
TERRY RYAN: Absolutely.

00:06:42.440 --> 00:06:45.090
One of the great things
about Google Cloud's default

00:06:45.090 --> 00:06:48.037
networking in our projects,
is if all the machines

00:06:48.037 --> 00:06:49.620
are in the same
project, all the VMs--

00:06:49.620 --> 00:06:52.710
the VMs are what are powering
the relaying and stamping here.

00:06:52.710 --> 00:06:54.937
If they're all in
the same project,

00:06:54.937 --> 00:06:57.270
then they are automatically
wired to talk to one another

00:06:57.270 --> 00:06:59.130
without the developer
having to do any work

00:06:59.130 --> 00:07:00.575
to make that happen.

00:07:00.575 --> 00:07:01.950
And so what that
allows you to do

00:07:01.950 --> 00:07:07.350
is get started without having
to set up VPNs and VPCs to get

00:07:07.350 --> 00:07:08.073
all that working.

00:07:08.073 --> 00:07:09.240
All of that is done for you.

00:07:09.240 --> 00:07:10.660
All the routing is done for you.

00:07:10.660 --> 00:07:12.870
And if you want, this is
an open-source project,

00:07:12.870 --> 00:07:15.840
so you can actually pull
down and see how we set it up

00:07:15.840 --> 00:07:18.230
and see that it really
is that super simple.

00:07:18.230 --> 00:07:20.220
JOANNA SMITH: And I like that it
makes it accessible to someone

00:07:20.220 --> 00:07:21.270
who might not have
that networking

00:07:21.270 --> 00:07:22.830
experience or
background, but so long

00:07:22.830 --> 00:07:24.330
as they can
understand the theory,

00:07:24.330 --> 00:07:25.250
they can actually get going.

00:07:25.250 --> 00:07:26.310
TERRY RYAN: Yeah, exactly.

00:07:26.310 --> 00:07:27.727
JOANNA SMITH: This
is so exciting.

00:07:27.727 --> 00:07:28.510
I love this demo.

00:07:28.510 --> 00:07:30.540
I am so happy to
take all my photos,

00:07:30.540 --> 00:07:32.880
and more importantly, the
fact that it is open source

00:07:32.880 --> 00:07:34.645
