00:08:31.650 --> 00:08:32.530
I'm Reto Meier.

00:08:32.530 --> 00:08:34.113
STEPHANIE WONG: And
I'm Stephanie Wong

00:08:34.113 --> 00:08:36.961
here with Amit Zavery, VP of
engineering, to dive into APIs.

00:08:36.961 --> 00:08:38.419
Welcome, and thanks
for joining us.

00:08:38.419 --> 00:08:39.820
AMIT ZAVERY: Sure,
thanks for having me.

00:08:39.820 --> 00:08:40.200
STEPHANIE WONG:
So I want to talk

00:08:40.200 --> 00:08:42.240
about APIs, which
have come a long way

00:08:42.240 --> 00:08:46.170
from the software interfaces
popularized by Win32 APIs.

00:08:46.170 --> 00:08:49.410
Tell us where they are today,
and your predictions for APIs

00:08:49.410 --> 00:08:51.950
in 2019 and where Apigee
lies in this space.

00:08:51.950 --> 00:08:52.950
AMIT ZAVERY: Sure, sure.

00:08:52.950 --> 00:08:55.240
I think if you look at what's
going on in the industry today,

00:08:55.240 --> 00:08:56.865
there are many things
which are driving

00:08:56.865 --> 00:08:59.310
the modernization of a lot
of existing applications.

00:08:59.310 --> 00:09:01.170
So APIs are becoming
the front end

00:09:01.170 --> 00:09:03.060
to a lot of the
assets companies have

00:09:03.060 --> 00:09:05.610
already in their
portfolio, and they unlock

00:09:05.610 --> 00:09:08.310
a lot of those content for
different kind of users,

00:09:08.310 --> 00:09:12.130
be it end users, be it
partners, as well as employees.

00:09:12.130 --> 00:09:14.760
So I think, if we look
at going forward in 2019,

00:09:14.760 --> 00:09:17.100
you'll start seeing a lot of
different ways of exposing

00:09:17.100 --> 00:09:18.130
those APIs.

00:09:18.130 --> 00:09:19.680
One, a lot of
companies are using,

00:09:19.680 --> 00:09:22.560
trying to monetize on
them to be able to use

00:09:22.560 --> 00:09:24.270
that to create a
lot of new content

00:09:24.270 --> 00:09:25.942
as well as partnerships.

00:09:25.942 --> 00:09:27.900
So we have a lot of good
examples of customers,

00:09:27.900 --> 00:09:30.408
say "Magazine Luisa"
in Brazil, who

00:09:30.408 --> 00:09:32.700
is trying to create a lot of
e-commerce kind of content

00:09:32.700 --> 00:09:36.150
and be able to expose a lot of
data to the end user to buy it.

00:09:36.150 --> 00:09:37.680
Same thing with
AccuWeather, where

00:09:37.680 --> 00:09:40.290
they want to be able to now
take a lot of the weather data

00:09:40.290 --> 00:09:43.530
and share it and make it
available to a lot of other end

00:09:43.530 --> 00:09:44.530
users as well.

00:09:44.530 --> 00:09:46.810
So that's one driving factor.

00:09:46.810 --> 00:09:50.220
Second thing is popularization
of a lot of ways of interfacing

00:09:50.220 --> 00:09:51.510
through voice and text.

00:09:51.510 --> 00:09:52.500
So you see bots.

00:09:52.500 --> 00:09:56.130
You see, of course, a different
kind of consumer devices,

00:09:56.130 --> 00:09:58.530
and that requires
a lot of interfaces

00:09:58.530 --> 00:10:00.870
into the back end systems
and be able to expose it

00:10:00.870 --> 00:10:02.050
through voice and text.

00:10:02.050 --> 00:10:03.960
So that's also driving
a lot of interest

00:10:03.960 --> 00:10:09.070
from companies to be able to
use API management for that.

00:10:09.070 --> 00:10:12.423
And third area, we've seen
a lot of driving factor,

00:10:12.423 --> 00:10:14.340
and you'll see a lot of
it as we move forward,

00:10:14.340 --> 00:10:16.640
is a lot of industry
requirements.

00:10:16.640 --> 00:10:18.640
So there's a lot of open
standards coming out,

00:10:18.640 --> 00:10:21.660
a lot of regulations coming
out, and APIs is the one way

00:10:21.660 --> 00:10:24.220
to log the content and
make sure they're secured

00:10:24.220 --> 00:10:26.590
and they are available
to the right people.

00:10:26.590 --> 00:10:29.700
So that's another
complete new requirement

00:10:29.700 --> 00:10:33.423
we're seeing and becoming very
popular as we move forward.

00:10:33.423 --> 00:10:34.590
RETO MEIER: That's exciting.

00:10:34.590 --> 00:10:37.260
Now, obviously, a lot of APIs
that drive ecosystems are

00:10:37.260 --> 00:10:40.710
publicly visible, but many of
the APIs at any given company

00:10:40.710 --> 00:10:43.050
are only used internally.

00:10:43.050 --> 00:10:45.320
How does Apigee support
both of those use cases?

00:10:45.320 --> 00:10:46.070
AMIT ZAVERY: Sure.

00:10:46.070 --> 00:10:49.130
So I think the way we have
built our platform at--

00:10:49.130 --> 00:10:50.760
in the Apigee area
was to really make

00:10:50.760 --> 00:10:53.460
sure that anybody who
wants to expose APIs,

00:10:53.460 --> 00:10:56.160
we provide them ways to
manage the whole process,

00:10:56.160 --> 00:10:58.660
be it internal or
external users.

00:10:58.660 --> 00:11:01.243
And so the platform is really
kind of agnostic of that fact,

00:11:01.243 --> 00:11:03.660
but we, of course, provide a
lot of safeguarding when it's

00:11:03.660 --> 00:11:06.660
externally exposed, a lot of
the right kind of regulations,

00:11:06.660 --> 00:11:09.630
the right kind of permissions,
security elements are built in.

00:11:09.630 --> 00:11:11.130
Similarly inside,
when you're trying

00:11:11.130 --> 00:11:12.810
to expose it inside
the enterprise,

00:11:12.810 --> 00:11:14.852
and be able to use it
between different employees

00:11:14.852 --> 00:11:16.435
for different
application development,

00:11:16.435 --> 00:11:18.780
again, we provide the whole
lifecycle management for it.

00:11:18.780 --> 00:11:20.530
So there are a lot of,
again, capabilities

00:11:20.530 --> 00:11:22.980
for a developer to
use and leverage

00:11:22.980 --> 00:11:24.690
when they're building
those applications,

00:11:24.690 --> 00:11:26.730
and they can decide the
roles and how and what

00:11:26.730 --> 00:11:29.100
is to be shared, and a lot
of features kind of build

00:11:29.100 --> 00:11:30.308
into really make that happen.

00:11:30.308 --> 00:11:31.892
STEPHANIE WONG: And
a lot of companies

00:11:31.892 --> 00:11:33.540
are now looking
towards microservices,

00:11:33.540 --> 00:11:35.165
but microservices
will continue to have

00:11:35.165 --> 00:11:36.480
a lot of different recipes.

00:11:36.480 --> 00:11:38.310
Successful microservice
architecture

00:11:38.310 --> 00:11:41.150
is complex to design,
build, and manage,

00:11:41.150 --> 00:11:43.390
and there are a lot of
experimentation going on.

00:11:43.390 --> 00:11:46.200
But the key is to build
reusable decoupled services that

00:11:46.200 --> 00:11:48.390
can scale with the right
levels of governance.

00:11:48.390 --> 00:11:51.790
How is Apigee helping to support
microservice architecture?

00:11:51.790 --> 00:11:53.290
AMIT ZAVERY: I
think, general, the--

00:11:53.290 --> 00:11:55.498
if you look at the architecture
evolution in general,

00:11:55.498 --> 00:11:59.070
it's idea is to really have
a small kind of services

00:11:59.070 --> 00:12:00.860
and decoupled in a way.

00:12:00.860 --> 00:12:04.560
And the best way to use those
services is through an API.

00:12:04.560 --> 00:12:07.350
So what Apigee does is,
now that we have interfaces

00:12:07.350 --> 00:12:10.530
to a lot of the microservices
management layer--

00:12:10.530 --> 00:12:14.280
for example, Istio, which
Google provides us a service,

00:12:14.280 --> 00:12:16.470
allows you to now expose
those microservices,

00:12:16.470 --> 00:12:18.090
manage them in a
much better way,

00:12:18.090 --> 00:12:21.930
and orchestrate and do a
lot of monitoring around it.

00:12:21.930 --> 00:12:24.660
And what we do with Apigee
is we integrate with Istio

00:12:24.660 --> 00:12:26.910
and really provide the
ability to manage those APIs

00:12:26.910 --> 00:12:28.090
through one of those--

00:12:28.090 --> 00:12:28.770
one way.

00:12:28.770 --> 00:12:31.380
Otherwise, if you have other
microservices, you have an API,

00:12:31.380 --> 00:12:34.423
we can, of course, manage
that lifecycle for that

00:12:34.423 --> 00:12:36.090
and allow you to use
those microservices

00:12:36.090 --> 00:12:38.590
in a much better fashion than
a monolithic application would

00:12:38.590 --> 00:12:39.102
be.

00:12:39.102 --> 00:12:39.810
RETO MEIER: Nice.

00:12:39.810 --> 00:12:42.330
Can you talk a little bit about
the new general availability

00:12:42.330 --> 00:12:44.472
releases of Apigee
Edge for private cloud?

00:12:44.472 --> 00:12:45.930
AMIT ZAVERY: Yeah,
I think, the way

00:12:45.930 --> 00:12:48.300
we're looking at
Apigee in general

00:12:48.300 --> 00:12:50.772
is to have a deployment
model which is flexible.

00:12:50.772 --> 00:12:52.230
So one is to be
able to, of course,

00:12:52.230 --> 00:12:54.330
you can continue
using it on prem.

00:12:54.330 --> 00:12:56.640
Second thing is to be able
to now take it in the cloud.

00:12:56.640 --> 00:12:59.490
We have an Apigee Edge product
is available in the cloud

00:12:59.490 --> 00:13:02.560
as well, fully
managed by Google.

00:13:02.560 --> 00:13:04.620
Third is this hybrid
mechanism, where

00:13:04.620 --> 00:13:07.520
you can have some of the runtime
running anywhere you choose, be

00:13:07.520 --> 00:13:10.330
it private cloud or on premise.

00:13:10.330 --> 00:13:12.620
You can be running
on Kubernetes or GKE.

00:13:12.620 --> 00:13:15.270
And the control plane
is running in the cloud,

00:13:15.270 --> 00:13:16.740
and that could be in any cloud.

00:13:16.740 --> 00:13:19.165
Could be Google or any
third party cloud as well.

00:13:19.165 --> 00:13:21.540
So we try to make sure the
architecture is very flexible,

00:13:21.540 --> 00:13:23.490
giving choices to the
customers and developers

00:13:23.490 --> 00:13:26.160
to really deploy the way they
would want to, and specifically

00:13:26.160 --> 00:13:29.130
with Edge, we do provide a
private cloud edition where you

00:13:29.130 --> 00:13:32.113
can run on GKE, and
GKE On-Premise can

00:13:32.113 --> 00:13:33.780
be available anywhere
the developer want

00:13:33.780 --> 00:13:35.130
to choose to deploy it.

00:13:35.130 --> 00:13:37.020
And we allow you to
have it very easy,

00:13:37.020 --> 00:13:39.180
and easy to manage
and easy to deploy.

00:13:39.180 --> 00:13:40.620
So I mean, that's
available today,

00:13:40.620 --> 00:13:42.570
and we continue to make
progress in that area.

00:13:42.570 --> 00:13:43.970
STEPHANIE WONG: It's exciting.

00:13:43.970 --> 00:13:45.870
So we know that
