00:30:13.450 --> 00:30:14.170
Hi, Priyanka.

00:30:14.170 --> 00:30:14.740
PRIYANKA VERGADIA:
Hi, Stephanie.

00:30:14.740 --> 00:30:15.800
Thank you for having me.

00:30:15.800 --> 00:30:16.883
STEPHANIE WONG: Thank you.

00:30:16.883 --> 00:30:18.430
Google has published
our practices

00:30:18.430 --> 00:30:20.890
for managing reliable
services at scale

00:30:20.890 --> 00:30:24.880
in site reliability engineering
books and this SRE workbook.

00:30:24.880 --> 00:30:26.440
One of the main
principles of SRE

00:30:26.440 --> 00:30:28.450
is using service
level objectives.

00:30:28.450 --> 00:30:30.820
Can you show us how
developers can do the same?

00:30:30.820 --> 00:30:32.110
PRIYANKA VERGADIA: Yeah,
let's dive right in and

00:30:32.110 --> 00:30:33.390
let me show it through a demo.

00:30:33.390 --> 00:30:33.940
STEPHANIE WONG: OK, great.

00:30:33.940 --> 00:30:35.200
PRIYANKA VERGADIA: Perfect.

00:30:35.200 --> 00:30:41.510
So I have this website here that
is built to order some stuff.

00:30:41.510 --> 00:30:44.610
And so I'm going to put
something in the cart.

00:30:44.610 --> 00:30:47.980
It's an e-commerce website,
so it lets you order things,

00:30:47.980 --> 00:30:49.370
and then it places an order.

00:30:49.370 --> 00:30:52.330
So I have my credit card
information in there,

00:30:52.330 --> 00:30:53.890
and we just placed an order.

00:30:53.890 --> 00:30:56.590
Now behind the scenes,
there is a lot going on

00:30:56.590 --> 00:30:58.820
in this particular application.

00:30:58.820 --> 00:31:00.980
So if we look at
what's going on here,

00:31:00.980 --> 00:31:03.250
this is the service
architecture of the application.

00:31:03.250 --> 00:31:05.340
And it is talking--
it has a front end

00:31:05.340 --> 00:31:07.630
and has various different
services talking

00:31:07.630 --> 00:31:10.240
to the front end, the checkout,
the catalog, the product

00:31:10.240 --> 00:31:12.280
catalog, the card service.

00:31:12.280 --> 00:31:13.970
There's a lot that's
going on here.

00:31:13.970 --> 00:31:17.050
And if you see further,
they're pretty much all

00:31:17.050 --> 00:31:20.270
written in very different
languages as well.

00:31:20.270 --> 00:31:22.300
So there's a lot of
complexity behind the scenes

00:31:22.300 --> 00:31:23.560
that's going on.

00:31:23.560 --> 00:31:27.260
And this is all done through
microservices, as you can see.

00:31:27.260 --> 00:31:32.550
So it's important to understand
that a simple website is set up

00:31:32.550 --> 00:31:35.590
with different-- can be set
up with different services

00:31:35.590 --> 00:31:37.990
in the back end and have
different languages that it

00:31:37.990 --> 00:31:38.810
can be written in.

00:31:38.810 --> 00:31:39.790
So complexities.

00:31:39.790 --> 00:31:40.180
STEPHANIE WONG: Right.

00:31:40.180 --> 00:31:41.740
And microservice
architectures mean

00:31:41.740 --> 00:31:44.650
the app itself is broken
into many small pieces,

00:31:44.650 --> 00:31:46.840
and doesn't that make
it hard to understand

00:31:46.840 --> 00:31:48.700
how all the pieces
fit together and where

00:31:48.700 --> 00:31:50.802
to start investigating
when there is a problem?

00:31:50.802 --> 00:31:52.510
PRIYANKA VERGADIA:
It's very-- it becomes

00:31:52.510 --> 00:31:54.200
very hard for the
operations team,

00:31:54.200 --> 00:31:55.990
especially in
microservices architecture,

00:31:55.990 --> 00:31:58.810
to figure out what's going
on, which services is up,

00:31:58.810 --> 00:32:00.580
down, talking to each other.

00:32:00.580 --> 00:32:04.570
So there's a good tool in
Stackdriver Monitoring called

00:32:04.570 --> 00:32:06.880
topology, where you
can actually look

00:32:06.880 --> 00:32:10.480
at the topology of your
different microservices

00:32:10.480 --> 00:32:11.450
talking to each other.

00:32:11.450 --> 00:32:15.820
So I have it open here, and when
you open the service topology,

00:32:15.820 --> 00:32:19.000
I can look at the specific
service that I'm interested in

00:32:19.000 --> 00:32:21.730
and then see which other
services it is talking to.

00:32:21.730 --> 00:32:23.500
So it gives me a view.

00:32:23.500 --> 00:32:27.580
I can click on one, and it gives
me a view of what's going on

00:32:27.580 --> 00:32:29.410
and which services
it is talking to,

00:32:29.410 --> 00:32:31.360
which is pretty
helpful when you're

00:32:31.360 --> 00:32:33.610
working in an
environment that has

00:32:33.610 --> 00:32:36.433
so many different microservices
that it's talking to.

00:32:36.433 --> 00:32:38.350
STEPHANIE WONG: Wow, and
this is great because

00:32:38.350 --> 00:32:40.390
with microservice-based
apps, you can clearly

00:32:40.390 --> 00:32:42.460
see the interservice
dependencies

00:32:42.460 --> 00:32:45.040
and use that to double
check new code rollouts.

00:32:45.040 --> 00:32:47.560
But let's say you're
setting up alerts.

00:32:47.560 --> 00:32:49.960
Ops staff can burn
out a lot of the times

00:32:49.960 --> 00:32:53.350
from getting notified of all the
potential causes of a problem,

00:32:53.350 --> 00:32:56.230
making it hard to align SREs
with customer interests.

00:32:56.230 --> 00:32:58.630
How can we identify which
behaviors really actually

00:32:58.630 --> 00:33:00.825
matter for that service
during service degradation?

00:33:00.825 --> 00:33:02.200
PRIYANKA VERGADIA:
Yeah, so there

00:33:02.200 --> 00:33:05.320
is also another tool within
Stackdriver Monitoring

00:33:05.320 --> 00:33:10.960
for services where you can
actually set up SLOs and define

00:33:10.960 --> 00:33:15.010
your SLOs for the services so
operations does not actually

00:33:15.010 --> 00:33:19.000
get affected by just looking at
all the different alerts that

00:33:19.000 --> 00:33:19.780
can come by.

00:33:19.780 --> 00:33:23.230
It's actually a practice
within our SRE teams

00:33:23.230 --> 00:33:28.390
where we make sure that our
SRE stuff is only monitoring

00:33:28.390 --> 00:33:32.020
alerts that are related to
customer issues and front end

00:33:32.020 --> 00:33:35.470
customer-related issues and not
every single issue that might

00:33:35.470 --> 00:33:38.490
be a cause to those issues.

00:33:38.490 --> 00:33:40.803
So let's see that
in the demo as well.

00:33:40.803 --> 00:33:41.720
STEPHANIE WONG: Great.

00:33:41.720 --> 00:33:44.320
PRIYANKA VERGADIA: So if
we click on Services here

00:33:44.320 --> 00:33:46.010
in the Stackdriver monitoring.

00:33:46.010 --> 00:33:47.840
Let's look at front end.

00:33:47.840 --> 00:33:52.630
And when we go do that, you're
able to see the service level

00:33:52.630 --> 00:33:53.610
optimization.

00:33:53.610 --> 00:33:56.020
So let me click on SLOs.

00:33:56.020 --> 00:33:59.110
And in SLOs you obviously
see nothing here.

00:33:59.110 --> 00:34:00.760
So we're going to set one up.

00:34:00.760 --> 00:34:04.450
And say we want to set
up for availability,

00:34:04.450 --> 00:34:05.800
and then there is--

00:34:05.800 --> 00:34:07.870
we can do it
rolling or calendar.

00:34:07.870 --> 00:34:10.900
I want to say in
the month, if we

00:34:10.900 --> 00:34:18.520
reach 99% or low availability,
then I want to get alerted.

00:34:18.520 --> 00:34:20.350
That's a good way of
saying that you're not

00:34:20.350 --> 00:34:21.889
going to be alerted
for everything,

00:34:21.889 --> 00:34:27.260
but you still want to be alerted
for when we go less than 99%.

00:34:27.260 --> 00:34:31.570
And then you can also
set up a latency alert.

00:34:31.570 --> 00:34:33.940
So in this case,
if we say latency--

00:34:33.940 --> 00:34:36.489
and I may want to
set up differently

00:34:36.489 --> 00:34:37.630
for different services.

00:34:37.630 --> 00:34:40.960
For the front end, you
could say my pages should

00:34:40.960 --> 00:34:43.989
load in less than
1,000 milliseconds,

00:34:43.989 --> 00:34:46.480
otherwise I want to be notified.

00:34:46.480 --> 00:34:51.520
And in this case also,
let's say it's 90% our goal.

00:34:51.520 --> 00:34:52.639
And then we submit that.

00:34:52.639 --> 00:34:54.306
So these are the two
alerts that we just

00:34:54.306 --> 00:34:57.330
set up for latency
and availability

00:34:57.330 --> 00:34:59.590
and they are not just
thresholds because we

00:34:59.590 --> 00:35:01.960
are saying this
percentile, so it's not

00:35:01.960 --> 00:35:03.750
going to be chatty and noisy.

00:35:03.750 --> 00:35:06.247
It's going to be
if this happens,

00:35:06.247 --> 00:35:08.580
then we really want to get
notified because a service is

00:35:08.580 --> 00:35:10.410
not working fine.

00:35:10.410 --> 00:35:12.900
So that's a good
way of identifying

00:35:12.900 --> 00:35:16.570
making sure that you're not
burning out your operations

00:35:16.570 --> 00:35:17.070
staff.

00:35:17.070 --> 00:35:18.153
STEPHANIE WONG: Excellent.

00:35:18.153 --> 00:35:20.610
How do you follow those best
practices given the resources

00:35:20.610 --> 00:35:21.770
that you have?

00:35:21.770 --> 00:35:22.770
PRIYANKA VERGADIA: Yeah.

00:35:22.770 --> 00:35:24.940
So you also have metrics
and diagnostics in there.

00:35:24.940 --> 00:35:27.000
So if you click
on Metrics, you're

00:35:27.000 --> 00:35:30.510
able to see the request counts
and the response latency

00:35:30.510 --> 00:35:33.483
is which will guide you through
the request and response.

00:35:33.483 --> 00:35:35.400
And if you click on
Diagnostics, that actually

00:35:35.400 --> 00:35:36.540
takes you to the trace.

00:35:36.540 --> 00:35:41.730
So now if I want to drill deeper
into the 95th percentile, which

00:35:41.730 --> 00:35:44.460
is probably the one I want
to look at because it's not

00:35:44.460 --> 00:35:46.740
performing very well,
then I can click on that

00:35:46.740 --> 00:35:51.517
and see a specific request why
it was slow, for what reasons,

00:35:51.517 --> 00:35:53.100
and which requests
were actually slow.

00:35:53.100 --> 00:35:55.800
So in this case, we can see
that the checkout service was

00:35:55.800 --> 00:35:58.020
a little bit slow
in send and receive,

00:35:58.020 --> 00:35:59.850
so we may want to
delve deeper into that

00:35:59.850 --> 00:36:01.510
and find out what's
going on there.

00:36:01.510 --> 00:36:03.990
So the Diagnostics tab
and the Metrics tab

00:36:03.990 --> 00:36:06.060
are really good friends
for troubleshooting.

00:36:06.060 --> 00:36:06.450
STEPHANIE WONG: Great.

00:36:06.450 --> 00:36:08.010
And at some point,
you probably need

00:36:08.010 --> 00:36:10.730
to dig a little deeper
into the services signals.

00:36:10.730 --> 00:36:12.720
Let's say maybe you
received an SLO alert

00:36:12.720 --> 00:36:14.850
and there's no obvious
upstream cause,

00:36:14.850 --> 00:36:17.610
or you have a customer complaint
outside of an SLO alert

00:36:17.610 --> 00:36:19.015
that you need to investigate.

00:36:19.015 --> 00:36:21.390
Or maybe you need to see how
the new rollout of your code

00:36:21.390 --> 00:36:22.440
version is going.

00:36:22.440 --> 00:36:24.930
How do you get a coherent
display of all the signals

00:36:24.930 --> 00:36:26.240
for a specific service?

00:36:26.240 --> 00:36:27.698
PRIYANKA VERGADIA:
Yeah, so I think

00:36:27.698 --> 00:36:29.490
the best thing to
do at that point

00:36:29.490 --> 00:36:32.220
is you got an alert through SLO.

00:36:32.220 --> 00:36:34.680
You want to look at
metrics and diagnostics

00:36:34.680 --> 00:36:36.510
to kind of figure
out what's going on,

00:36:36.510 --> 00:36:37.980
because metrics
and diagnostics are

00:36:37.980 --> 00:36:40.050
going to be helpful
to understand

00:36:40.050 --> 00:36:42.330
which specific service
is not performing well.

00:36:42.330 --> 00:36:45.268
As you can see in diagnostics,
when we drilled deeper

00:36:45.268 --> 00:36:47.310
into a specific service,
we were able to find out

00:36:47.310 --> 00:36:49.980
that checkout is the culprit,
and so we can drill deeper

00:36:49.980 --> 00:36:50.570
into that.

00:36:50.570 --> 00:36:53.820
So those metrics and diagnostics
would be your great friends

00:36:53.820 --> 00:36:56.998
in order to find out
what's actually going on.

00:36:56.998 --> 00:36:59.040
STEPHANIE WONG: So
Stackdriver Service Monitoring

00:36:59.040 --> 00:37:01.123
gives you a whole new way
to view your application

00:37:01.123 --> 00:37:03.900
architecture, reason about
its customer-facing behaviors,

00:37:03.900 --> 00:37:05.858
and get to the root of
any problem that arises.

00:37:05.858 --> 00:37:07.775
So thank you so much,
Priyanka, for showing us

00:37:07.775 --> 00:37:10.470
how Google is transforming the
ops experience of Cloud native

00:37:10.470 --> 00:37:11.730
and microservice deployments.

00:37:11.730 --> 00:37:13.522
PRIYANKA VERGADIA:
Thank you for having me.

00:37:13.522 --> 00:37:14.023
It was fun.

00:37:14.023 --> 00:37:15.940
STEPHANIE WONG: Time now
for our next DevByte.

00:37:15.940 --> 00:37:18.315
If you want to learn how to
build amazing apps with Cloud

00:37:18.315 --> 00:37:20.968
Firestore, check this out.

00:37:20.968 --> 00:37:23.010
NATALIE VAIS: Let's say
you want to build an app.

00:37:23.010 --> 00:37:25.470
Not just any app, but
a serverless, scalable,

00:37:25.470 --> 00:37:28.420
mobile, syncing,
analytical, IoT app.

00:37:28.420 --> 00:37:30.000
If you do a quick
Google search, you

00:37:30.000 --> 00:37:33.150
will get 1.2 billion
results on how to do this.

00:37:33.150 --> 00:37:34.630
So where do you start?

00:37:34.630 --> 00:37:36.870
My name is Natalie Vais,
and I'm a product manager

00:37:36.870 --> 00:37:38.430
on Cloud Firestore.

00:37:38.430 --> 00:37:41.730
Today, I want to talk to you
about building amazing apps,

00:37:41.730 --> 00:37:43.980
specifically how
to build globally

00:37:43.980 --> 00:37:46.170
scalable apps with
Cloud Firestore.

00:37:46.170 --> 00:37:48.060
If you're starting a
new project from scratch

00:37:48.060 --> 00:37:49.560
and you want to
build it yourself,

00:37:49.560 --> 00:37:52.120
you'll likely have to think
about a couple of things.

00:37:52.120 --> 00:37:54.630
Number one, setting
up a database.

00:37:54.630 --> 00:37:58.170
Number two, creating some kind
of API layer on top of it,

00:37:58.170 --> 00:38:00.450
so you can make queries
from your clients.

00:38:00.450 --> 00:38:03.570
And that probably means third,
creating an authentication

00:38:03.570 --> 00:38:06.270
service to make sure
users can only access

00:38:06.270 --> 00:38:07.680
data they're supposed to.

00:38:07.680 --> 00:38:09.750
And then lastly,
making sure your data

00:38:09.750 --> 00:38:11.730
syncs with your
local results, which

00:38:11.730 --> 00:38:14.640
are probably being stored in
a completely separate client

00:38:14.640 --> 00:38:16.000
database.

00:38:16.000 --> 00:38:19.140
Now, just make sure it works
across multiple platforms

00:38:19.140 --> 00:38:21.240
and can easily handle
things like spotty network

00:38:21.240 --> 00:38:22.230
connections.

00:38:22.230 --> 00:38:23.670
Simple, right?

00:38:23.670 --> 00:38:25.500
As soon as your app goes viral--

00:38:25.500 --> 00:38:27.000
fingers crossed for you--

00:38:27.000 --> 00:38:29.430
all of a sudden you have
to worry about scaling,

00:38:29.430 --> 00:38:32.370
high availability, and
disaster recovery strategies

00:38:32.370 --> 00:38:35.910
and you'll probably need to hire
more people to manage it, too.

00:38:35.910 --> 00:38:38.730
Or you can just use
Cloud Firestore.

00:38:38.730 --> 00:38:41.280
Cloud Firestore is
a fully managed,

00:38:41.280 --> 00:38:43.920
serverless, NoSQL
document database,

00:38:43.920 --> 00:38:46.170
purpose built for solving
some of the toughest

00:38:46.170 --> 00:38:50.250
problems of mobile, web, and
IoT app development today.

00:38:50.250 --> 00:38:52.630
In many ways, it's
more than a database.

00:38:52.630 --> 00:38:55.320
It's an entire back
end for your apps.

00:38:55.320 --> 00:38:57.840
To create Firestore,
we learned from two

00:38:57.840 --> 00:39:01.170
other popular databases at
Google, Firebase Realtime

00:39:01.170 --> 00:39:03.720
Database and Cloud Datastore.

00:39:03.720 --> 00:39:06.360
Usage patterns have shifted
since those architectures

00:39:06.360 --> 00:39:09.640
were designed some 7 to 10
years ago, respectively.

00:39:09.640 --> 00:39:12.390
So we took a close look at the
developer wish list for each

00:39:12.390 --> 00:39:14.430
of these to inform the product.

00:39:14.430 --> 00:39:16.470
You can think of
Firestore as the best

00:39:16.470 --> 00:39:18.290
of both of these worlds.

00:39:18.290 --> 00:39:20.455
Cloud Firestore
handles all the things

00:39:20.455 --> 00:39:22.080
that you don't want
to think about when

00:39:22.080 --> 00:39:23.800
you're developing a mobile app.

00:39:23.800 --> 00:39:27.480
So you can really focus
on your core business.

00:39:27.480 --> 00:39:30.810
The first thing to know is
that Cloud Firestore is fully

00:39:30.810 --> 00:39:33.330
supported in the Firebase SDKs.

00:39:33.330 --> 00:39:36.180
This includes web,
iOS, and Android,

00:39:36.180 --> 00:39:39.480
so you can access data
directly from these clients.

00:39:39.480 --> 00:39:43.020
This works in conjunction with
Firebase Auth and Security

00:39:43.020 --> 00:39:43.680
Rules.

00:39:43.680 --> 00:39:45.840
And those things
allow you to control

00:39:45.840 --> 00:39:49.070
who has access to read and
modify data on the server.

00:39:49.070 --> 00:39:52.960
There's that off layer we
mentioned at the beginning.

00:39:52.960 --> 00:39:55.830
You wouldn't believe how
normal it has become for people

00:39:55.830 --> 00:39:57.960
to look at their apps
when there is absolutely

00:39:57.960 --> 00:40:00.170
no chance of connectivity.

00:40:00.170 --> 00:40:04.050
A person checks their phone an
average of 80 times in a day,

00:40:04.050 --> 00:40:06.390
so it's becoming increasingly
common for people

00:40:06.390 --> 00:40:10.350
to expect our apps to work
offline and across devices.

00:40:10.350 --> 00:40:13.110
With Cloud Firestore,
the client libraries

00:40:13.110 --> 00:40:16.380
already come with full
support for offline mode.

00:40:16.380 --> 00:40:19.380
The SDK handles conflict
resolution on device

00:40:19.380 --> 00:40:22.170
and syncs up with the
server when you reconnect.

00:40:22.170 --> 00:40:26.080
Because we built Cloud Firestore
with a serverless architecture,

00:40:26.080 --> 00:40:28.680
you don't have to worry
about provisioning, capacity

00:40:28.680 --> 00:40:30.900
planning, scaling,
or maintenance.

00:40:30.900 --> 00:40:33.660
There are servers
distributed across the world,

00:40:33.660 --> 00:40:36.480
just not ones that
you have to manage.

00:40:36.480 --> 00:40:38.760
All of this is taken
care of by the system.

00:40:38.760 --> 00:40:42.630
Scaling is automatic and
happens as an online operation.

00:40:42.630 --> 00:40:45.540
Say goodbye to
maintenance windows.

00:40:45.540 --> 00:40:49.630
On top of that, Cloud Firestore
is built for the Cloud.

00:40:49.630 --> 00:40:52.260
That means it's replicated
across zones and regions

00:40:52.260 --> 00:40:55.050
depending on how you
choose to configure it.

00:40:55.050 --> 00:40:58.260
In this year alone, and as part
of going generally available,

00:40:58.260 --> 00:41:01.320
we added 10 new locations
to Cloud Firestore.

00:41:01.320 --> 00:41:04.140
Replication across
data centers is really

00:41:04.140 --> 00:41:06.870
one of those things you want
to outsource if you can.

00:41:06.870 --> 00:41:08.610
It becomes complicated
quickly and is

00:41:08.610 --> 00:41:11.160
a huge overhead for ops teams.

00:41:11.160 --> 00:41:15.360
With this replication, you get
five-nines SLA for multi-region

00:41:15.360 --> 00:41:18.280
and a four-nines SLA for
regional deployments.

00:41:18.280 --> 00:41:21.780
This is one of the highest
availability services we offer.

00:41:21.780 --> 00:41:24.630
All of this lets you focus
on your core business

00:41:24.630 --> 00:41:27.900
and leave the infrastructure
to Google Cloud, which

00:41:27.900 --> 00:41:29.520
is our core business.

00:41:29.520 --> 00:41:32.710
With over 1 million
databases created already,

00:41:32.710 --> 00:41:35.520
Cloud Firestore has
seen a huge diversity

00:41:35.520 --> 00:41:38.550
of workloads already deployed
within our customer database.

00:41:38.550 --> 00:41:40.890
The unifying themes
among these workloads

00:41:40.890 --> 00:41:44.190
include number one, the
need for data access

00:41:44.190 --> 00:41:46.980
even when connectivity
lapses, number two,

00:41:46.980 --> 00:41:50.070
scalability for many
users across platforms,

00:41:50.070 --> 00:41:52.320
and number three, the
ability to move quickly

00:41:52.320 --> 00:41:54.430
from prototype to production.

00:41:54.430 --> 00:41:57.450
And that's Cloud Firestore,
your autosyncing,

00:41:57.450 --> 00:41:59.640
autoscaling database
in the cloud.

00:41:59.640 --> 00:42:02.420
Give it a try, and find out why
it's the most discussed cloud

00:42:02.420 --> 00:42:03.615
database on Stack Overflow.

