00:43:02.975 --> 00:43:03.220
RAE WANG: Thank you.

00:43:03.220 --> 00:43:04.137
It's great to be here.

00:43:04.137 --> 00:43:06.720
STEPHANIE WONG: So security is
such a vital part of everything

00:43:06.720 --> 00:43:08.070
we do online and in the cloud.

00:43:08.070 --> 00:43:09.960
Let's talk about
security at scale.

00:43:09.960 --> 00:43:13.210
As our environments grow larger,
security simply must keep up.

00:43:13.210 --> 00:43:14.880
How is network
security changing as we

00:43:14.880 --> 00:43:17.400
move from brittle environments
built on static IPs used

00:43:17.400 --> 00:43:19.183
to microservices?

00:43:19.183 --> 00:43:21.600
RAE WANG: Yeah, the traditional
IP-based networking policy

00:43:21.600 --> 00:43:23.260
requires a very
detailed knowledge

00:43:23.260 --> 00:43:27.390
on the nitty-gritty parts of
your networking configurations.

00:43:27.390 --> 00:43:29.970
They don't really offer you
a concept of which service

00:43:29.970 --> 00:43:33.330
can talk to what unless you
segregate them by IP addresses

00:43:33.330 --> 00:43:34.950
and subnets ahead of time.

00:43:34.950 --> 00:43:36.810
And this quickly
becomes unwieldy, right?

00:43:36.810 --> 00:43:38.590
As you scale your
infrastructure,

00:43:38.590 --> 00:43:41.430
especially as you introduce
the concept of microservices.

00:43:41.430 --> 00:43:44.810
And this is where a
cloud-native platform like Istio

00:43:44.810 --> 00:43:47.370
or the managed version, CSM,
give a very different kind

00:43:47.370 --> 00:43:48.330
of experience.

00:43:48.330 --> 00:43:51.090
They allow the admins to
define high level policies that

00:43:51.090 --> 00:43:54.555
focus on application
requirements of business logic.

00:43:54.555 --> 00:43:57.330
The microservices are backed
by namespaces, which give you

00:43:57.330 --> 00:44:00.860
a natural isolation
boundaries, and each service

00:44:00.860 --> 00:44:03.930
is also given a unique
Istio service identity.

00:44:03.930 --> 00:44:06.510
Then you can set policies
leveraging these identities

00:44:06.510 --> 00:44:07.690
and boundaries.

00:44:07.690 --> 00:44:10.250
For example, you can set
to be zero trust by default

00:44:10.250 --> 00:44:12.570
between the services, so
that any communication will

00:44:12.570 --> 00:44:14.280
require explicit policies.

00:44:14.280 --> 00:44:17.130
And then mutual TLS is
enforced by default.

00:44:17.130 --> 00:44:19.470
And once you have
configured the policies,

00:44:19.470 --> 00:44:23.310
CSM can allow you to centrally
manage and monitor and control

00:44:23.310 --> 00:44:26.860
service-to-service traffic and
alert on any policy violation.

00:44:26.860 --> 00:44:29.125
So this is how whether
microservice focused a platform

00:44:29.125 --> 00:44:32.040
like CSM, it's much
simpler to both create

00:44:32.040 --> 00:44:35.907
and do ongoing monitoring of
network security policies.

00:44:35.907 --> 00:44:38.490
RETO MEIER: So how does moving
into a hybrid cloud environment

00:44:38.490 --> 00:44:39.725
impact security?

00:44:39.725 --> 00:44:40.350
RAE WANG: Yeah.

00:44:40.350 --> 00:44:42.142
So moving to hybrid
environment, especially

00:44:42.142 --> 00:44:43.980
with a product
like Anthos, right,

00:44:43.980 --> 00:44:48.240
means you now have a consistent
control plane between on-prem

00:44:48.240 --> 00:44:49.020
and cloud.

00:44:49.020 --> 00:44:51.270
It gives you a single
admin experience

00:44:51.270 --> 00:44:54.420
in a single pane of glass, which
makes it much simpler to have

00:44:54.420 --> 00:44:57.935
central visibility and control
across the hybrid deployments.

00:44:57.935 --> 00:44:59.310
And then you can
also start using

00:44:59.310 --> 00:45:01.920
some of the Google
Security capabilities,

00:45:01.920 --> 00:45:03.420
even for your on-prem workloads.

00:45:03.420 --> 00:45:08.550
For example, products like
binary authorization or Anthos

00:45:08.550 --> 00:45:11.250
config management now
work for on-prem as well.

00:45:11.250 --> 00:45:14.310
And of course, another benefit
of using hybrid platform

00:45:14.310 --> 00:45:17.370
is that helps your workloads
to get cloud-ready, right?

00:45:17.370 --> 00:45:20.430
Because containers are
portable and the admin

00:45:20.430 --> 00:45:21.340
experience the same.

00:45:21.340 --> 00:45:22.860
So when it comes
time to migrate,

00:45:22.860 --> 00:45:24.820
it's a very
straightforward process.

00:45:24.820 --> 00:45:27.150
And of course, once your
workloads migrate to cloud,

00:45:27.150 --> 00:45:29.580
we can offer you
additional security values,

00:45:29.580 --> 00:45:32.850
things like shielded VMs,
or zero downtime patching

00:45:32.850 --> 00:45:35.550
through live migration,
even when there are really

00:45:35.550 --> 00:45:38.675
big hardware vulnerabilities
like Spectre and Meltdown.

00:45:38.675 --> 00:45:40.800
STEPHANIE WONG: You mentioned
security consistency.

00:45:40.800 --> 00:45:42.258
What are some of
the best practices

00:45:42.258 --> 00:45:44.550
to ensure a security
baseline with consistency

00:45:44.550 --> 00:45:47.378
and reproducibility for all of
your resources in the cloud?

00:45:47.378 --> 00:45:48.420
RAE WANG: Great question.

00:45:48.420 --> 00:45:51.820
We know that security is kind of
a shared responsibility, right?

00:45:51.820 --> 00:45:54.300
We do our part, and
customers do their parts.

00:45:54.300 --> 00:45:57.570
So in a platform, we offer
a lot of security capability

00:45:57.570 --> 00:46:00.640
so that customers can start
on a much higher baseline.

00:46:00.640 --> 00:46:02.820
But at the same time,
we also make sure that--

00:46:02.820 --> 00:46:05.040
we also want to make sure
your part of responsibility

00:46:05.040 --> 00:46:06.930
becomes easy as well,
and that's often

00:46:06.930 --> 00:46:09.720
done through templatized
deployments, central config

00:46:09.720 --> 00:46:11.820
and policy management,
an ongoing monitoring

00:46:11.820 --> 00:46:12.880
and learning.

00:46:12.880 --> 00:46:16.740
So then in terms of deployments,
by using Kubernetes style API,

00:46:16.740 --> 00:46:19.050
you can express
your configurations

00:46:19.050 --> 00:46:20.970
as declarative templates.

00:46:20.970 --> 00:46:23.190
And those config
management gave you

00:46:23.190 --> 00:46:25.680
a central source of
truth for these templates

00:46:25.680 --> 00:46:27.840
and allow you to deploy
the config and policy

00:46:27.840 --> 00:46:31.380
updates by GitOps and
[? CSC ?] best practice.

00:46:31.380 --> 00:46:33.712
These changes then get
deployed across all

00:46:33.712 --> 00:46:35.670
of the clusters and
measures in the environment

00:46:35.670 --> 00:46:37.340
to ensure consistency.

00:46:37.340 --> 00:46:39.720
And then when it comes
to constraints, a couple

00:46:39.720 --> 00:46:44.160
frameworks-- gatekeeper, policy
controller, and Forseti Config

00:46:44.160 --> 00:46:46.410
Validators that we are
developing the open source

00:46:46.410 --> 00:46:48.870
with communities,
they allow admins

00:46:48.870 --> 00:46:51.330
to standardize
configurations across

00:46:51.330 --> 00:46:52.770
your entire organization.

00:46:52.770 --> 00:46:54.990
You can, for example,
set guardrails

00:46:54.990 --> 00:46:57.342
by presubmit checks in
your release pipeline.

00:46:57.342 --> 00:46:58.800
So that misconfiguration
can't even

00:46:58.800 --> 00:47:00.670
get into the deployment process.

00:47:00.670 --> 00:47:04.220
And then in case drift
happens in production,

00:47:04.220 --> 00:47:07.100
you can also set alerts
and then get notified.

00:47:07.100 --> 00:47:10.230
For monitoring, we also have
Cloud Asset Inventory and Cloud

00:47:10.230 --> 00:47:11.910
Security Command Center.

00:47:11.910 --> 00:47:14.676
They give you a central
view of the state of all

00:47:14.676 --> 00:47:16.845
of your resources in
production, and then

00:47:16.845 --> 00:47:19.220
can notify you of changes and
risks and security findings

00:47:19.220 --> 00:47:20.530
and so on.

00:47:20.530 --> 00:47:22.050
So together, these
tools help you

00:47:22.050 --> 00:47:25.950
through the lifecycle of
managing your security config

00:47:25.950 --> 00:47:26.572
settings.

00:47:26.572 --> 00:47:27.780
RETO MEIER: That's fantastic.

00:47:27.780 --> 00:47:30.420
Can you talk a little bit
about declarative configs

00:47:30.420 --> 00:47:34.200
to make security controls
consistent across scale

00:47:34.200 --> 00:47:35.380
diverse environments?

00:47:35.380 --> 00:47:36.060
RAE WANG: Yeah.

00:47:36.060 --> 00:47:39.270
So we all know one of
the most costly mistakes

00:47:39.270 --> 00:47:41.870
an admin can make is
misconfiguration, right?

00:47:41.870 --> 00:47:44.510
It's almost far too easy to
accidentally set your IAM

00:47:44.510 --> 00:47:48.590
controls to be public or make
your network configuration

00:47:48.590 --> 00:47:49.955
to be way too permissive.

00:47:49.955 --> 00:47:52.330
And when you do that, you open
up your sensitive resource

00:47:52.330 --> 00:47:55.070
to attacks and data leaks.

00:47:55.070 --> 00:47:58.960
So this is where declarative
config makes a difference

00:47:58.960 --> 00:48:01.460
because it helps the admin to
focus on the end goal, the end

00:48:01.460 --> 00:48:02.270
state.

00:48:02.270 --> 00:48:05.900
And then allow to offload the
intermediate orchestration

00:48:05.900 --> 00:48:08.030
and workflows and
keeping up consistencies

00:48:08.030 --> 00:48:09.770
related to the platform.

00:48:09.770 --> 00:48:12.740
And then when we do that
across a lot of resources,

00:48:12.740 --> 00:48:14.900
across many clusters
and measures,

00:48:14.900 --> 00:48:18.290
we basically achieve
reproducibility and recovery

00:48:18.290 --> 00:48:21.260
from drift, and therefore
they reduce the complexity

00:48:21.260 --> 00:48:23.215
of applying security controls.

00:48:23.215 --> 00:48:24.590
And they also make
it much easier

00:48:24.590 --> 00:48:27.770
to share security best
practices because you can

00:48:27.770 --> 00:48:29.353
deploy templatized solutions.

00:48:29.353 --> 00:48:30.770
STEPHANIE WONG:
And not only is it

00:48:30.770 --> 00:48:32.480
important to declare
security configs

00:48:32.480 --> 00:48:35.680
across your environments, but
also monitor inevitable risks.

00:48:35.680 --> 00:48:37.430
So we have a big launch,
as you mentioned,

00:48:37.430 --> 00:48:39.045
Cloud Security Command Center.

00:48:39.045 --> 00:48:40.670
How do we help
customers get visibility

00:48:40.670 --> 00:48:42.248
into risk in their environments?

00:48:42.248 --> 00:48:44.540
RAE WANG: Yeah, we're very
excited about Cloud Security

00:48:44.540 --> 00:48:48.020
Command Center because it
kind of unifies the security

00:48:48.020 --> 00:48:50.150
detections from Google,
together with a lot

00:48:50.150 --> 00:48:51.860
of the key third
party detections

00:48:51.860 --> 00:48:53.600
for the main essential
place, and really

00:48:53.600 --> 00:48:56.720
help the admins to build them,
analyze them, and take actions

00:48:56.720 --> 00:48:58.230
in place.

00:48:58.230 --> 00:49:00.350
For example, one
of the capabilities

00:49:00.350 --> 00:49:03.380
is Security Health Analytics.

00:49:03.380 --> 00:49:06.080
It scans and detects over
20 common vulnerabilites

00:49:06.080 --> 00:49:09.110
from publicly exposed
storage buckets to,

00:49:09.110 --> 00:49:11.360
we talk about overly
permissive firewall rules,

00:49:11.360 --> 00:49:14.590
to misconfigured Kubernetes
clusters and so on.

00:49:14.590 --> 00:49:17.840
Similarly, you have
Cloud Anomaly Detection.

00:49:17.840 --> 00:49:20.030
It automatically detects
some of the key threats

00:49:20.030 --> 00:49:23.600
from cryptojacking
to exposed service

00:49:23.600 --> 00:49:25.940
credentials to outbound DDoS.

00:49:25.940 --> 00:49:28.490
And then with this GA release,
another benefit you get

00:49:28.490 --> 00:49:32.240
is now Cloud SCC unifies
security findings

00:49:32.240 --> 00:49:34.970
from a variety of
Google Cloud products,

00:49:34.970 --> 00:49:37.850
from event threat detection
to binary authorization

00:49:37.850 --> 00:49:42.038
to cloud asset inventory
to [? XT ?] and so on.

00:49:42.038 --> 00:49:42.830
How you can use it?

00:49:42.830 --> 00:49:44.963
Well, if you're
in a press company

00:49:44.963 --> 00:49:46.380
and you have a
finance department,

00:49:46.380 --> 00:49:48.200
for example, that
stores credit card

00:49:48.200 --> 00:49:50.180
information and
sensitive, personal data,

00:49:50.180 --> 00:49:53.780
you can use Cloud SCC to
scan the infrastructure used

00:49:53.780 --> 00:49:56.210
for storing these sensitive
information, identify

00:49:56.210 --> 00:49:59.030
any vulnerabilities, as well
as finding active threats that

00:49:59.030 --> 00:50:00.852
are targeting these
vulnerabilities.

00:50:00.852 --> 00:50:02.310
And then there's
a lot of findings.

00:50:02.310 --> 00:50:05.690
So we give you a way to manage
and organize these findings

00:50:05.690 --> 00:50:08.690
by attaching a business
context to the findings

00:50:08.690 --> 00:50:11.870
and the resources via
[? atcote ?] security marks.

00:50:11.870 --> 00:50:13.700
And then we all know
that getting the data

00:50:13.700 --> 00:50:14.780
is the first step.

00:50:14.780 --> 00:50:17.363
What you really want to do is
to improve your security posture

00:50:17.363 --> 00:50:17.970
from the data.

00:50:17.970 --> 00:50:20.630
So Cloud SCC also allows
you to actually respond

00:50:20.630 --> 00:50:24.290
to take actions by, for example,
triggering alerts or creating

00:50:24.290 --> 00:50:27.920
IARM instance or directly
executing remediation

00:50:27.920 --> 00:50:29.343
operations via Cloud Functions.

